  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 237 AM EDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nToday through Friday Night...\n\nFairly similar conditions to previous days are present across the \narea this morning, with temperatures ranging from around 40 degrees \nin the coldest valleys to the mid-to-upper 50s in urban areas and \nhigher elevations as of 06Z. Satellite imagery shows a relative \nminimum in high cirrus clouds over the area this morning; however, \nthese should increase again through the day, bringing filtered \nsunshine later today. From now through mid-morning, the main impact \nwill again be the development of fog in river valleys and other \nsheltered areas. While some of this could be locally dense, coverage \nshould remain confined enough to keep impacts to a minimum.\n\nAs previous discussions have noted, steady/persistent conditions are \nexpected through Friday. The synoptic pattern will feature mean \ntroughing across the western CONUS with ridging across the southeast \nUS. A few shortwave disturbances ejecting across the Plains and into \nthe Great Lakes region will bring extra mid- and high clouds and \nslightly stronger SW winds through the mid-week period; however, dry \nair below 20k ft AGL will preclude precipitation chances. High \ntemperatures each afternoon will be in the mid-to-upper 70s for \nmost, and a few locations may approach 80 Thursday and Friday. Large \ndiurnal ranges will allow temperatures to fall into the 40s and low \n50s each night until Friday night, when increasing moisture should \nhold temperatures in the 50s.\n\nThe main weather impact through Friday will be increasing \nprecipitation deficits and worsening drought conditions across the \narea, as has been well covered in previous discussions. Recent \ncollaboration with area fire weather partners revealed that small \nfuels humidity values aren't too concerning thanks to good nighttime \nrecovery of atmospheric RH. Additionally, the combination of light \nwinds and intermittent high clouds limiting afternoon mixing should \nkeep fire weather conditions fairly tame over the next several days.\n\nSaturday through Monday Night...\n\nThe static weather pattern is expected to come to an end this \nweekend, with a shift in the large-scale pattern gradually taking \nplace between Saturday and the middle of next week. The main western \nUS troughing is expected to remain fairly fixed through the first \nhalf of the weekend; however, energy associated with the remnants of \nTS Vance (E. Pacific) should get caught up in SW flow across the \ncentral CONUS and eject into the Mississippi Valley Saturday into \nSunday. As this disturbance gets pulled toward our region, moisture \nwill increase as a plume of 1.25\"+ PWATs advects in from the \nsouthwest. Most of the day on Saturday still looks dry at this time, \nwith precipitation chances increasing from SW to NE during the \nevening hours. Still, the highest ensemble probabilities (60-70%) of \nmeasurable rainfall arrive after midnight into Sunday morning. \nOverall, this system should bring fairly light rain amounts, with \ncurrent ensemble median values ranging from 0.10-0.40\" across the \nCWA.  \n\nThis lead disturbance should eject to the east by the end of the \nweekend, with the main mid-latitude trough ejecting across the \nPlains and toward the eastern CONUS early next week. There is still \nquite a bit of spread with how this wave evolves, resulting in a \nwide range of weather outcomes for early-to-mid next week. The \ngeneral signals in medium-range guidance support a more active \nweather pattern with additional chances for rain (possibly storms) \nduring the first few days of November. Temperatures are expected to \ngradually trend cooler after Monday as well as lower \nheights/troughing moves toward the area.",
